#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/id #uuid "0021ae6a-8ef8-564e-9c2e-fb5f9e8f7313" :question/math-academy-id "e-464" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 2 \\sin {\\left(\\dfrac x 3\\right)} \\,  \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\dfrac x 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac 1 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =3\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068916 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/id #uuid "daf5cc74-4c7d-500a-9345-03bd6b73a333" :question/math-academy-id "e-465" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 5 \\csc {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\cot {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\,  \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 6\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =\\dfrac 1 6\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068917 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "234c7a86-037c-5316-9419-14db90d1da60" :question/math-academy-id "e-466" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\sec^2\\left(x^3-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u = x^3 - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 3x^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u = x^2\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "03e461a3-0fe0-5fb5-813e-ae23a5dc606a" :question/math-academy-id "e-467" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/4} \\dfrac{\\tan x \\sec^2 x}{1+\\tan^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068917 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bba42d0-4d22-55d1-bf2a-5afa7f154545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266532} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0dba1e-ebd9-58df-9ff0-a39f534c6156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267118} {:answer/id #uuid "9a78f2b9-be60-5dd9-a1b2-d3dae91c37e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267540} {:answer/id #uuid "a54496d0-737c-5f0e-b323-1106d3723e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267634} {:answer/id #uuid "da3d8464-42f7-59e9-bbc9-ba3a5c255583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bba42d0-4d22-55d1-bf2a-5afa7f154545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266532} :answer-field/id #uuid "464dd787-aac1-5fd3-8b68-a426f70499c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d3c5f6c-be88-5a7d-88c2-f5f9124aa97d" :question/math-academy-id "q-13533" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{\\pi /2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201f04d6-c002-5e0e-95fe-a7bfb6f3cd47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266427} {:answer/id #uuid "4017a737-6567-579a-8f02-98712b431a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266723} {:answer/id #uuid "928bb7be-da0b-5934-b68c-38f79005663e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267450} {:answer/id #uuid "932c94aa-6652-5204-910b-18fdb3861f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267461} {:answer/id #uuid "99faa052-1151-586c-8bf2-0746b9af0da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4017a737-6567-579a-8f02-98712b431a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266723} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6fc5f0-4245-5250-93b5-36ad6b67d400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e8b61bc-bb4e-5bb5-ab6c-973e00e2baa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-13647" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068917 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a0832f3-44e5-55dc-b0d6-cde9d7754977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a0832f3-44e5-55dc-b0d6-cde9d7754977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317931} :answer-field/id #uuid "81013bc8-e699-5fa8-92f3-108172fec02c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4977c738-ef65-5d72-9699-ec8683cb5818" :question/math-academy-id "q-350203" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi /12}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /6}}12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=6{x}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=12x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=12x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\sqrt{\\pi /12}$ | $\\sqrt{\\pi /6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /2$ | $\\pi $ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi /12}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /6}}12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi /12}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /6}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6{x}^{2})\\cdot 12x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u)\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0-1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb74e6d-27ba-5dab-9bc3-14bcd6aca19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb74e6d-27ba-5dab-9bc3-14bcd6aca19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319487} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb778354-29e8-5b6b-9ab2-f74bbde5e184" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8cafdf18-d2d8-5608-a2ba-c48b80df9a64" :question/math-academy-id "q-350904" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{4}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{4}^{3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
